# Keep the build context clean — and, more importantly, keep a LOCAL docker build
# producing the same image CI does.
#
# Every Python service Dockerfile ends with a broad source copy, e.g.
#   COPY services/agent-runtime/ ./
# which lands AFTER `uv sync` has built /app/.venv. On a developer machine that
# copy overwrites the freshly built venv with the host's services/<svc>/.venv, and
# the container ends up with a venv whose interpreter symlink points at the
# developer's laptop:
#
#   /app/.venv/bin/python ->
#     /Users/<someone>/.local/share/uv/python/cpython-3.12-macos-aarch64-none/bin/python3.12
#
# The symlink is unresolvable in the container, so a bare `python` silently falls
# through PATH to the system interpreter, which has none of the service's
# dependencies — every import of datacern_common fails. It went unnoticed because
# the images ran `uv run ...`, and uv quietly rebuilds a broken venv on each
# start; that repair is impossible once the pod is non-root on a read-only root
# filesystem, which is when it finally surfaced.
#
# CI never hit this: .venv is gitignored, so actions/checkout produces a tree
# without one. That divergence is the real hazard — a local build could not
# reproduce a CI image, and a local build was quietly broken in a way no test
# covered.
.venv/
**/.venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc

# Git metadata and local tooling: large, and nothing in a runtime image needs it.
.git/
.github/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/node_modules/
**/.next/

# Local state that must never reach an image.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/*.log
